<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Subject–Verb Agreement - Find the subject</h1>
  <section class="summary-block summary-blue">
    <h2>Ask who or what</h2>
    <p>Ask “Who or what is doing this?” or “Who or what is being described?” Ignore a prepositional phrase and other extra detail.</p>
    <p>In “The necklace with three bright beads <strong>is</strong> mine,” the head noun is <em>necklace</em>, so the subject is singular.</p>
    <p>In “The beads on the necklace <strong>are</strong> bright,” the subject is <em>beads</em>, so it is plural.</p>
    <p>The <strong>bag</strong> of apples <strong>is</strong> heavy. The <strong>apples</strong> in the bag <strong>are</strong> fresh.</p>
    <p>A <strong>friend</strong> of my parents <strong>lives</strong> nearby. My <strong>friends</strong> from school <strong>live</strong> nearby.</p>
  </section>
  <div class="trap-card trap-compact">
    <section class="trap">
      <h2>The subject is not inside the phrase</h2>
      <p>
        The basket of apples <strong>are</strong> heavy.
        <span class="mark mark-no" aria-label="Incorrect">✕</span>
      </p>
      <p>
        The <strong>basket</strong> of apples <strong>is</strong> heavy.
        <span class="mark mark-yes" aria-label="Correct">✓</span>
        You are weighing the basket as one thing.
      </p>
      <p>The apples would need <strong>are</strong>. If you spread them on the table, you say, “The apples are heavy.”</p>
    </section>
  </div>
  <section class="summary-block summary-mint">
    <h2>More phrases to ignore</h2>
    <p>The group <strong>of boys is</strong> loud.</p>
    <p>The box <strong>of toys has</strong> fallen.</p>
  </section>
</div>
